module bindings.java.netty_fast_thread_local_instance_field;

// A netty FastThreadLocal does not hash: its constructor takes a global index
// from InternalThreadLocalMap.nextVariableIndex(), a counter that only ever
// increments, and every FastThreadLocalThread sizes its indexed-variables
// array to the largest index any FastThreadLocal has claimed. The slot is held
// for the life of the instance and is never returned, which the class's own
// design states plainly -- enough claimed indices and nextVariableIndex itself
// throws "too many thread-local indexed variables".
//
// That makes the number of FastThreadLocal constructions the resource, and a
// field initializer is the spelling that multiplies it: `new FastThreadLocal<>()`
// in a non-static field initializer runs once per construction of the enclosing
// instance, so a class built per connection, per pool or per session converts
// ordinary churn into growth of every event-loop thread's array, with no bound
// on either axis. The static spelling claims one slot for the class however
// often it is instantiated, which is why the same declaration with the static
// modifier is the remediated form and reports nothing here.
//
// The instance_field_init token is the discriminator the modifiers alone never
// provided: it is emitted for a field initializer that constructs the named
// type and carries whether that construction is per class or per instance.
// The residual is the constructor-assigned variant -- a FastThreadLocal-typed
// instance field with no initializer, built in the constructor body, holds no
// initializer for the walk to see and does not match.
binding perInstanceFastThreadLocal {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "class"
    and node.context.instanceFieldInit in ["FastThreadLocal"]
  emit issue code.UnboundedAllocationReview at node
}
